## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical skills in per-prefix top-K maintenance using a Trie with bounded heaps and dynamic frequency updates, revealing trade-offs between speed and memory.

## Key Concepts
- Trie with per-node top-K storage using a min-heap with bounded size K
- Min-heap with tie-breaking by word for stable ordering
- Dynamic frequency updates with addWord(word, delta) supporting negative deltas
- Automatic deletion when frequency reaches zero

## Time and Memory Trade-offs
- **Time Complexity**: addWord runs in O(L × log K) where L is word length, as we traverse the path and perform heap operations. getTopK runs in O(K × log K) for heap extraction plus O(K log K) for sorting.
- **Space Complexity**: O(N × L + N × K) where N is the total number of unique words—the Trie itself costs O(N × L), and each node stores up to K heap entries.
- **Trade-off**: Increasing K improves result quality but increases memory usage and slows updates. A smaller K is faster but returns fewer suggestions.

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.topK = new MinHeap((a, b) => {
      if (a.freq !== b.freq) return a.freq - b.freq;
      return a.word.localeCompare(b.word);
    });
  }
}

class AutocompleteEngine {
  constructor(K = 3) {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
    this.freqMap = new Map();
    this.K = K;
  }

  addWord(word, delta) {
    const newFreq = (this.freqMap.get(word) || 0) + delta;
    if (newFreq <= 0) {
      this.freqMap.delete(word);
    } else {
      this.freqMap.set(word, newFreq);
    }

    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) {
        node.children.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.children.get(char);
      node.topK.upsert({ word, freq: newFreq }, this.K);
    }
  }

  getTopK(prefix, K) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const char of prefix) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) return [];
      node = node.children.get(char);
    }
    return node.topK.getTop(K);
  }
}
```

## Purpose
This problem tests sliding-window aggregation with per-key state management, lazy expiration strategies, and top-K extraction using a heap. It integrates multiple data structures: hashtables for O(1) key lookups, per-key queues for temporal ordering, and a heap for maintaining ranked sums.

## Key Concepts
- **Sliding window maintenance**: Events expire once they exceed the window width W
- **Per-key accumulation**: Each key maintains its own queue of (timestamp, value) pairs
- **Lazy eviction**: Stale entries are removed only when encountered, avoiding unnecessary cleanup
- **Amortized efficiency**: The cost of expiration is distributed across active operations

## Code Example
```javascript
class WindowedTopKMap {
  constructor(windowWidth) {
    this.W = windowWidth;
    this.map = new Map();
    this.heap = new MinHeap();
  }

  addEvent(key, value, ts) {
    if (!this.map.has(key)) {
      this.map.set(key, { sum: 0, queue: [] });
    }
    const entry = this.map.get(key);
    entry.queue.push({ ts, value });
    entry.sum += value;
    this.heap.push({ sum: entry.sum, key });
  }

  advanceTime(now) {
    // Lazy cleanup handled in topKKeys
  }

  topKKeys(K, now) {
    const results = [];
    while (results.length < K && !this.heap.isEmpty()) {
      const top = this.heap.pop();
      const entry = this.map.get(top.key);
      if (!entry) continue;
      this._expire(entry.queue, now);
      if (Math.abs(entry.sum - top.sum) > 0.0001) continue;
      results.push(top.key);
    }
    return results;
  }

  _expire(queue, now) {
    while (queue.length && queue[0].ts <= now - this.W) {
      entry.sum -= queue.shift().value;
    }
  }
}
```

